{
  "dom_id": "step-q332566",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z\\ge -2.06).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0162\\,$ | $\\,0.0158\\,$ | $\\,0.0154\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0207\\,$ | $\\,0.0202\\,$ | $\\,0.0197\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0262\\,$ | $\\,0.0256\\,$ | $\\,0.0250\\,$ |\n\n$P(Z\\ge -2.06)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z\\ge -2.06).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14254773/assets/30c8380c76e236d13780e3fd6f9dc2b5a76f825ac55411befc3d7fddf07c452d.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSince the total area under the graph equals $1,$ we can express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.06) & =1-P(Z<-2.06) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.06).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14254773/assets/6a1cfc6c71b551cdde6880007fc1eee1cb439a0e32443a406dee0fdac3f112e4.png)\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-2.06)=0.0197.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.06) & =1-Φ(-2.06) \\\\  & =1-0.0197 \\\\  & =0.9803.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
